#instruction
You are a senior formal-methods engineer.

Your task is to translate the natural-language requirements into a **single,
syntactically valid SMT-LIB 2.6 program** that is already tagged for
UNSAT-core analysis. The file must parse without errors in Z3.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Strict output contract
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. Prepend **exactly one** line  
   (set-logic ALL)  
   at the very top.  
   Do **NOT** emit (check-sat) or (exit).

2. **VARIABLE comments (NEW)**  
   • For **every** declaration in the VARIABLES block  
     `(declare-sort …)`, `(declare-const …)`, `(declare-fun …)`,  
     `(define-sort …)`, `(define-fun …)` –  
     append a right-hand comment beginning with `;` that explains, in
     clear prose, what the symbol represents.  
     Example:  
     ```smt
     (declare-const hasMetastaticDisease (Patient Bool)) ; True if the patient has metastatic disease
     ```

3. **Tagging rule (MANDATORY)**  
   Wrap every assertion in `(! … :named req<i>_<j>)`, where  
      • `<i>` = requirement number from its header,  
      • `<j>` = counter starting at 1 for that requirement.

4. Declare every sort, constant, or function you use; prefer standard theories
   (`Int`, `Real`, `Bool`, `BitVec`, `Arrays`, etc.).

5. **Respect the skeleton in #input**:  
   fill the VARIABLES block and replace each placeholder assertion with
   well-typed, logically correct formulas.  
   You may add extra tagged assertions but must not remove or renumber headers.

====================================================================
Final output format — **output nothing else**
====================================================================
<output_complete_smt_program>
  …the complete, two-banner SMT-LIB program exactly as described above…
</output_complete_smt_program>

#input
<!-- Optional global context that applies to all requirements -->
<contextual_text>
#CONTEXTUAL_TEXT#
</contextual_text>

<!-- Skeleton with REQUIREMENT headers and placeholder assertions -->
<req_blocks>
#REQ_BLOCKS#
</req_blocks>

<!-- SMT created in earlier iterations (may be blank) -->
<smt_program_by_far>
#SMT_PROGRAM_BY_FAR#
</smt_program_by_far>
